# Post-processing + orthographic camera showcase: Wave C features
# (MASTER-PLAN parity rows 3 and 30, plus true instancing, row 22).
# One still frame (no animation): identical spheres at three very
# different depths, a few other primitives scattered in depth, and a
# row of cubes copied from one base cube -- all seen through an
# orthographic camera and finished with an ordered filter chain.
# Run: python3 tools/f3dc/f3dc.py run examples/post-ortho.f3d

scene "post-ortho" size 640 480 background black

# --- Wave C: orthographic camera (row 3) ---------------------------
# `camera ortho` replaces the default perspective projection with a
# parallel one; `size N` is the height of the view volume in world
# units. Under ortho an object keeps the same screen size no matter
# how far away it is -- compare the three identical spheres below.
# The camera is placed explicitly, on the sphere row's axis and
# looking straight at its centre (at 0 1 12, look 0 1 0), so the
# whole row sits in the middle of the frustum instead of drifting
# to its edge (and into the vignette) under the diagonal default
# camera; size 8.5 keeps every sphere fully inside the frame.
camera ortho size 8.5 at 0 1 12 look 0 1 0

# --- Wave C: post-processing filter chain (row 30) -----------------
# `filter` is a scene-level statement. Filters run in the order they
# are written, as a chain over the finished frame, so reordering
# these lines changes the result: bloom first (bright parts are
# lifted), then vignette (edges darken), then gamma (brightness curve).
filter bloom 0.6
filter vignette 0.4
filter gamma 1.2

light directional dir -0.5 -1 -0.7 strength 1.2
light ambient strength 0.15
plane ground gray at 0 -2 0 size 16

# Distance-independence, the point of ortho: three spheres with the
# SAME radius (1) at near, middle and far depths along the view
# direction (z = 4, 0, -4, i.e. 8, 12 and 16 units from the camera),
# spread across the frame (x = -4, 0, 4) so none of them overlaps
# or is clipped. A perspective camera would draw the far one much
# smaller; under `camera ortho` all three come out the same size.
sphere depth_near red at -4 2.5 4 radius 1
sphere depth_mid green at 0 2.5 0 radius 1
sphere depth_far blue at 4 2.5 -4 radius 1

# Other primitives at varied depths, for the same comparison.
cube mid_cube orange at 0 0 -3 size 2
cylinder near_pillar cyan at 3 -1 -6 radius 1 height 3
pyramid far_pyramid purple at 4 0 5 size 2 height 2

# --- Wave C: instancing --------------------------------------------
# One base cube, defined once like any other object ...
cube base_cube yellow at -4 -1.5 4 size 1
# ... and three `instance` copies of it in a row along the front.
# Each instance names a NEW object, says which existing object it
# copies (`of base_cube`), and gives its own placement; `rotate`
# (degrees, x,y,z) and `scale` are optional per-instance overrides,
# as row_cube_2 (turned) and row_cube_3 (shrunk) show.
instance row_cube_1 of base_cube at -2,-1.5,4
instance row_cube_2 of base_cube at 0,-1.5,4 rotate 0,45,0
instance row_cube_3 of base_cube at 2,-1.5,4 scale 0.7

render
